From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giulio Milani <giulio.milani99@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:14:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Mac: read wired Xbox 360 pads over USB, with no driver

macOS has no driver for the Xbox 360 pad, so GameController never lists it, and it has no HID interface, so pad_hid_mac.h never sees it. A wired pad was not a player at all.

pad_xusb_mac.h claims the pad's XUSB interface (ff/5d/01) on a device of class ff/ff/ff from the host's own process, reads its 20-byte input report with one pending interrupt read on the main run loop, and hands it to controllers_mac.m the way a generic HID pad is handed over: its own slot, the shared button map, focus and unplugging. No kernel extension, DriverKit extension or entitlement is needed. Only a vendor-specific device is opened, so a hub, a disk or a keyboard is never touched. pad_xusb.h decodes the report (buttons by position, the shared round dead zone, a trigger past half is ZL / ZR) and is checked without a pad by tests/t_pad_xusb.c.

Checked on a PDP 0e6f:0301 on macOS 27.2: every button, the D-pad, both triggers and both sticks to full deflection, the rest drift absorbed, and the pad unplugged while read. Not read: the wireless receiver and the Xbox One pads (ff/47/d0, another protocol), rumble, the player light, the Guide button.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#2436
Reason: wired Xbox 360 pads have no macOS driver, so two-player games could not use a second pad
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#1197
